  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened institutional capacity of workers&apos; organizations</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The trade union movement in Bangladesh continues to face structural, legal and political constraints that limit effective organization, collective bargaining and representation&#x2014;particularly amid high labour informality and low union density (4.9%). As the country advances toward LDC graduation and a middle-income trajectory, strengthening labour institutions and inclusive social dialogue remains critical for ensuring decent work and balanced economic transformation.
In this context, ILO supports efforts to build a more cohesive, representative and forward-looking labour movement. Building on the National Coordination Council for Workers&#x2019; Education (NCCWE) strategic plan (2023&#x2013;2026), the focus is shifting toward strengthening unity, expanding representation to non-organized workers, and enhancing the role of trade unions in emerging policy areas.
Key priorities include developing a joint action framework among trade unions to consolidate positions on national labour issues and reduce fragmentation; empowerment of youth and women in trade union leadership; supporting strategies to organize informal, platform and other non-traditional workers; and strengthening collaboration through a Trade Union&#x2013;Civil Society Action Alliance (TUCSAA) to support reintegration of returnee migrant workers and contribute to a National Action Plan on Labour Migration.
ILO will also support trade unions to engage in Just Transition dialogues, ensuring workers&#x2019; perspectives are reflected in climate and sectoral policies. Additionally, practical tools and resource materials will be developed to enhance unions&#x2019; capacity to support migrant reintegration, including access to skills and employment services.
These efforts aim to foster a more unified, inclusive and influential trade union movement capable of shaping labour policy, advancing workers&#x2019; rights and contributing to sustainable and equitable development in Bangladesh.</narrative>
  </description>
